SECTION synthesis-scope
NUMBER 11.1
TITLE "Partial versus Final Synthesis"

GOAL
  Partial-Synthesis runs before Exit A only for an explicitly bounded
  persona/cluster/track/snapshot; Final-Synthesis runs only after Composite Exit A
  passes.
END

ENUM GAP-MATERIALITY
  Material
  Supporting
END

ENUM GAP-DISPOSITION
  Pending
  Ticket-Created
  Frontier-Created
  Both-Created
  Merged
  Invalid
  Approved-Excluded
END

FIELD SYNTHESIS-GAP
  source-synthesis-invocation-block-id            -> string required
  observed-missing-requirement                    -> string required
  affected-ids                                    -> string required
  source-fingerprints-snapshot                    -> string required
  proposed-target-persona-cluster-track-pov-ledger -> string required
  dedup-key                                       -> hash required
  materiality                                     -> GAP-MATERIALITY required
  disposition                                     -> GAP-DISPOSITION required
  resulting-ticket-frt-dec-ids                    -> string required
END

RULE partial-vs-final-synthesis
  GOAL
    Partial-Synthesis blocks remain B-DRAFT, are labeled provisional, and state the
    incomplete denominator. They MUST NOT be reconstruction inputs. Final-Synthesis
    writes complete structured catalogs from closed facts. Newly discovered gaps go to
    SYNTHESIS-GAP-BUFFER.md; Sequential Reconciliation opens tickets, creates or updates
    missing SRC/frontier/coverage records, immediately marks the pinned Exit A report
    invalid, and marks all dependent B blocks stale. Deconstruction is re-armed and
    confirmation/handoff is prohibited until Exit A passes again on a new gate-input
    fingerprint. Final synthesis cannot directly mutate ledgers.
  END
END

RULE cross-reference-updates
  GOAL
    After IDs exist, Cross-Reference-Reconciliation may update only fields such as Bound
    Use-Cases, Rules, Entities, Interfaces, and reciprocal references. It MUST NOT
    change observed behavior, decisions, or evidence; because these references are
    semantic payload, a changed reference increments the semantic record
    version/fingerprint and invalidates any certification envelope.
  END
END

RULE gap-buffer-merge
  GOAL
    Partial/Final Synthesis writes only Pending GAP records and cannot create tickets or
    frontiers. Sequential Reconciliation sorts by DEDUP-KEY, then GAP-ID; merges equal
    keys while retaining every source invocation/block/fingerprint; records a
    contradiction rather than merging incompatible target proposals; and
    deterministically creates the minimum required work. A missing cross-POV answer
    creates one canonical ticket in the proposed ledger; a newly discovered traversal
    boundary creates one FRT; a gap requiring both creates both and cross-links them.
    Existing equivalent active work is linked rather than duplicated. Sequential
    Reconciliation updates disposition atomically, invalidates the pinned Exit A report
    for material gaps, applies targeted staleness, and logs iteration-budget effects
    under §10.6.
  END
END

SECTION synthesis-block-header
NUMBER 11.2
TITLE "Common synthesis block header"

GOAL
  Every atomic synthesis block MUST contain a semantic payload followed by a separately
  delimited certification envelope.
END

FIELD SYNTHESIS-BLOCK-HEADER
  semantic-record-version      -> int required
  semantic-content-fingerprint -> string required
  coverage-status              -> string required
  derived-from                 -> string required
  material-claims              -> string required
  block-confidence-rank         -> string required
  block-evidence-profile       -> string required
  supersession                 -> string required
END

FIELD CERTIFICATION-ENVELOPE
  blueprint-status               -> string required
  confirmation                   -> string required
  confirmation-envelope-version  -> int required
  confirmation-envelope-audit    -> string required
END

RULE common-header-requirements
  GOAL
    Coverage is semantic payload; blueprint status is envelope metadata. B-CONFIRMED
    requires C-COVERED, no stale dependency, a passing candidate binding, and a valid
    human confirmation of the exact semantic version/fingerprint. Attaching confirmation
    MUST NOT change semantic bytes. Empty domains require inventory-backed zero claims
    and may then be confirmed as empty.
  END
END

SECTION architecture-blueprint
NUMBER 11.3
TITLE "Architecture blueprint — `90-ARCH-BLUEPRINT.md`"

GOAL
  The architecture blueprint contains system boundary and context diagrams;
  actors/personas and external systems; observed legacy technology separated from target
  decisions; atomic MOD records; inter-module contracts and error propagation;
  deployment topology; architectural invariants and modernization consequences;
  capability navigation; and a prominent active-versus-disabled architecture view.
  Purely navigational module indexes are grouping containers under §4.2 and list MOD IDs
  only.
END

FIELD ARCHITECTURE-MODULE
  responsibility-domain-boundary      -> string required
  public-interfaces                   -> string required
  encapsulated-state-entities         -> string required
  dependencies-dependents             -> string required
  data-and-events-passed              -> string required
  invariants-rules-owned              -> string required
  personas-use-cases-capabilities     -> string required
  failure-propagation                 -> string required
  observed-legacy-placement           -> string required
  target-decision                     -> string required
END

RULE module-requirements
  GOAL
    Every semantic architecture statement about responsibility, public interface,
    encapsulation, dependency, or data passing MUST belong to an atomic MOD and have
    material CLMs. MOD to IF/ER/DEP/BR/UC/CAP/FLT reciprocal references are required
    where applicable.
  END
END

